summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Follow-up of chronic problems.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Carolyn W., is a 49-year-old female, who presents today for follow-up of her chronic problems.

The patient reports that her osteoarthritis has been flaring up a little bit lately. She states that it is primarily in her elbows and it is really bothering her. She has been sitting at the computer, at the desk more in the office, so she does not know if it is being stagnant in that position all day which has increased her flare-ups for that, but she wanted to talk to me about that. She reports that she is right-handed. She states that she is typing all day versus writing. She denies any other joint pain. She reports that she does have some numbness in her hands when she is typing for long periods of time, she tries to shake out her arms a little bit to relieve that sensation. She states that she has had this history of gout. The last episode she had was about 3 months ago, she had some inflammation of her toe. She states that the medication I gave her really controlled it and she has not seen a flare up since. She reports that her psoriasis has been under control too. She states that it has not been a major problem for her either. She states that she was given some clobetasol for her scalp and that is doing a lot better.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Musculoskeletal: Reports osteoarthritis, primarily in her elbows.
• Neurological: Denies lower extremity edema.

VITALS

• Vital signs are within normal limits.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• Constitutional: In no apparent distress.
• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.
• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs.
• Gastrointestinal: Soft and non-tender. No hepatosplenomegaly.
• Musculoskeletal: Examination of the right elbow: Edema and inflammation of the right olecranon. Tenderness and effusion present. Pain with palpation of the olecranon bursa. Pain with pronation and supination. Pain with flexion and extension of the right arm.
• Skin: No rash or lesions.

RESULTS

X-rays of the right elbow: No significant findings.

ASSESSMENT

• Gout, stable.
• Osteoarthritis of the right elbow.
• Psoriasis, stable.

PLAN

I have examined the patient and discussed her current issues with her today. She is a 49-year-old female who presents today for follow-up of her chronic problems. She is doing well with her psoriasis. She is doing well with her gout. She is having some right elbow pain. She has been having some osteoarthritis pain in her elbows. She had an x-ray of her right elbow today. Her x-ray of the right elbow was normal. I have recommended that we refer her to physical therapy. I have prescribed her meloxicam 15 mg once a day. I have also recommended that we get an x-ray of her right elbow.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in 6 weeks.

Physician Name: Dr. Smith
