summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Rebecca Wilson is a 27-year-old female who presents for the evaluation of right knee pain. The onset of her pain began 1 week ago and has been present over the course of a month. The pain is localized to the right knee and is only present while swimming. She denies any injury. She has been swimming regularly over the past month and uses the breaststroke. She is able to swim 10 laps before the pain begins. She denies any pain radiating down her leg.

MEDICAL HISTORY

The patient has a history of carpal tunnel release 2 years ago and chronic back pain. She also has a history of depression.

SOCIAL HISTORY

The patient reports that she has been sleeping well. She has been eating well and denies any current suicidal or homicidal ideation. She has been participating in therapy for her depression and has been compliant with her medication. She has been trying to get outside more and this has been helping her.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Musculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain, denies pain radiating down the leg, denies swelling in joints.
• Neurological: Denies numbness or tingling.
• Psychological: Denies depression, anxiety, or mood swings.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.
• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs.
• Gastrointestinal: Soft, non-tender. No hepatosplenomegaly.
• Musculoskeletal: Examination of the right knee: No swelling in the joints. Pain with patellar inhibition. Pain with knee range of motion. No tenderness to palpation.
• Neurological: Cranial nerves II-XII grossly intact. Motor exam: 5/5 in upper extremities. 5/5 in lower extremities. Reflexes 2+ bilaterally. Sensory exam: Intact to light touch and pinprick. No dysesthesias.
• Endocrine: Thyroid: No thyromegaly. No bruit or bruit over the neck.

RESULTS

X-ray of the right knee was reviewed and is unremarkable.

ASSESSMENT

• Chondromalacia of the patella.
• Chronic back pain, stable.
• Depression, stable.

PLAN

For her right knee pain, I discussed the diagnosis of chondromalacia of the patella with the patient. I explained that this is due to overuse from her increased swimming activity. I recommended that she take ibuprofen 600 mg 4 times a day with food. I also advised her to perform straight leg raising exercises to strengthen her quadriceps. I advised her to avoid any activities that put extra pressure on her knee for a little while. I advised her to avoid breaststroke and to take a break from swimming if needed. We will see her again in a couple of weeks if her pain does not improve.

For her chronic back pain, I recommended that she continue to swim. This has been well-tolerated and I do not want to make any changes at this time.

For her depression, I recommended that we continue her current medication regimen. She has been doing well with her therapy and I do not want to make any changes at this time.

For her history of carpal tunnel release, I recommended that we continue to monitor her. She reports that she have been doing well and I do not want to make any changes at this time.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 2 weeks if her pain does not improve.
