summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Hypertension and palpitations.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Mr. Jordan Roberts is a 49-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for an initial consultation. The patient reports that he has a history of hypertension and palpitations. He states that his blood pressure has been elevated since he was young and he has had palpitations in the past. The patient notes that he was recently in the emergency room with high blood pressure and palpitations. He states that he believes his symptoms were related to panic attacks, as he had consumed multiple cups of coffee and had not eaten breakfast. The patient reports that he has not experienced these symptoms since.

The patient reports that he has been experiencing his blood pressure. He states that he is on a new medication that his primary care physician placed him on one month ago. The patient reports that he is currently taking Benicar HCT 40-25 and Amlodipine 10 mg daily. The patient states that he checks his blood pressure daily and his readings have been between 146-155 mmHg. He denies that his blood pressure is below 120 mmHg. The patient reports that he is trying to reduce his salt intake and is walking on the treadmill when he can.

FAMILY HISTORY

The patient reports that he has a family history of hypertension.

MEDICATIONS

The patient reports that he is taking Amlodipine 10 mg daily and Benicar HCT 40-25.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Cardiovascular: Reports palpitations.
• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.
• Gastrointestinal: Denies nausea or vomiting.

PHYSICAL EXAMINATION

• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, rubs, or gallops.
• Respiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally.
• Musculoskeletal: No edema.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Mr. Jordan Roberts is a 49-year-old male who presents with resistant hypertension. He is on three medications and maximum dose. He is currently on Benicar HCT 40-25 and Amlodipine 10 mg daily. We will add Spironolactone. We will monitor his potassium and sodium levels. We will order a metabolic panel and blood pressure check in 2 weeks. Next medication would be carvedilol, which I would like to avoid due to potential for erectile dysfunction. I have discussed the potential side effects of Spironolactone with the patient, including the potential for gynecomastia and electrolyte disturbances. If he is not controlled on Spironolactone, the next step would be to send him to the resistant hypertension clinic.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>
