summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Follow-up for adult hydrocephalus.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Christopher Watson is an 86-year-old male who presents today for his first visit since undergoing shunt surgery on 08/01/2020. I have followed the patient since 05/2020 when he first presented with 8-10 years of progressive gait impairment, cognitive impairment, and decreased bladder control. We established a diagnosis of adult hydrocephalus with a spino-catheter protocol in 06/2020, and he underwent shunt surgery on 08/01/2020. A Medtronic Strata programmable shunt in a ventricular peritoneal configuration was programmed at a level of 2.0 and placed.

The patient reports that he has been doing well since his shunt surgery. He has noticed improvement in his walking ability and cognitive function. He is now able to walk to the dining room, which he was unable to do before. He does still have intermittent difficulty with walking, but his daughter reports that he is doing much better than he was prior to surgery. The patient reports that his bladder control improved after surgery, but has since plateaued and he is now wearing a diaper. He denies any sores or pain with the use of the diaper. The patient also reports that he is doing better cognitively. He states that he does not feel lost when talking to someone anymore. His daughter reports that his improvement has been noticeable, stating that she feels she can have a normal conversation with him again.

The patient denies any headaches or pain at the site of the shunt. He also denies any abdominal pain.

PAST HISTORY

Medical
Adult hydrocephalus.

SURGICAL HISTORY

Shunt surgery on 08/01/2020.

SOCIAL HISTORY

The patient is a retired mechanic. He lives in a rural setting with his daughter. He enjoys watching TV and playing cards.

FAMILY HISTORY

Father: Deceased.

MEDICATIONS

None.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies fever or unintentional weight loss.
GU: Reports urinary incontinence and wearing a diaper. Denies genital sores, decreased or painful output.
Neurological: Reports walking impairment and cognitive impairment. Denies headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations.
Psychiatric: Denies depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings.

VITALS

BP: 124/80
HR: 64
Respirations: 18
Weight: 174 lbs
Pain: 0/10

PHYSICAL EXAM

Neurological: The patient is a pleasant and cooperative male who is able to converse easily through his daughter. The shunt site was clean, dry, and intact with a confirmed setting of 2.0. He was tested for recent and remote memory, attention span, and concentration in fund of knowledge. He scored 26/30 on the MMSE when tested with spelling and 25/30 when tested with calculations. He was able to get 2 of 3 memory words with cuing and the third with multiple choice. This was a slight improvement over his initial score of 23/30 with calculations and 24/30 with spelling. He was unable to remember any memory words with cuing without and only one with multiple choice at the time of the previous test.

Gait testing using the Tinetti assessment tool. He was tested without an assistive device and received a gait score of 6-8/12 and a balance score of 12/16 for a total score of 18-20/28. This is slightly improved from his initial score of 15-17/28.

Cranial nerves: PERRLA, EOMI, face symmetric, no dysarthria. Motor: Normal bulk and strength. Coordination: Slow from nose to finger.

RESULTS

CT from head 10/15/2020 shows a frontal horn span at the level of the foramen of Monro of 4.6 cm with a third ventricular contour that is flat with a span of 10 mm. By my reading there is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with a tiny subdermal collection. This was not noticed or noted by the radiologist who stated any extra-axial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.

ASSESSMENT

Christopher Watson is a 86-year-old male with a significant past medical history of adult hydrocephalus, gait impairment, urinary incontinence, and cognitive impairment. He presents today for follow-up of his shunt. The patient has made some improvement since his shunt surgery. He is able to walk to the dining room, which he was unable to do before. He is also doing better cognitively. He is still having some difficulty with walking and has plateaued with bladder control. He denies any headaches or pain at the site of the shunt.

His CT shows a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with a tiny subdermal collection. This was not noticed or noted by the radiologist who stated any extra-axial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.

Plan
1. We will leave the shunt set for another 2-3 months before we consider changing it. The patient is doing well and there are no symptoms of shunt malfunction.
2. I discussed with the patient that it is unlikely that he will be able to drive again. He is doing better but his movement speed is still not at the level needed to pass a driver's safety evaluation. He could definitely enroll in a driver safety program without my recommendation, but I would be happy to recommend it if he continues to improve over the next 9-12 months.
3. We will continue to monitor his improvement over the next 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>
