summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Follow-up for hypertension.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

The patient, Amanda Taylor, is a 72-year-old female who presents for follow-up for hypertension. Her history includes significant alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia. At her last visit on 04/16/2021, her blood pressure was 130/90. After presenting at 150/100, she was prescribed hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg by mouth once daily and lisinopril 20 mg by mouth once daily. CMP and CBC were essentially unremarkable.

The patient reports that she is struggling with not drinking. She would like to discuss the shot that was mentioned last time, but they did not get to discuss it too much.

She reports that she last had a drink on Monday. She has gone a couple of days without it. She has been drinking for a while. She reports drinking 4-5 times a week, hard alcohol. She is taking her blood pressure medications, but she is only taking half of the lisinopril. She reports that the first time she took it she felt lethargic and tired, so she only took a half dose the next time. She felt much better and she did not have the headaches or the "swoshing" that started in her left ear. She feels like it is definitely helping but she does not know it is not going to be okay until she stops drinking.

She reports that she is taking the full dose of the hydrochlorothiazide. She denies chest tightness or pressure or shortness of breath. She is doing blood pressure checks at home. Her morning blood pressure is 130-145/95-100. She reports that her blood pressure is higher in the evening, but it goes down after a while. She is taking her medicine in the morning when she gets up, not in the evening.

PAST HISTORY

Medical
Alcohol use disorder
Mild hypercholesterolemia

SOCIAL HISTORY

Alcohol 4-5 times a week to excess
No tobacco
No substance abuse

CURRENT MEDICATIONS

Lisinopril 10 mg daily
Hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Denies fever, weight loss, or weight gain.
• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath or cough.
• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or palpitations.
• Neurological: Denies headaches, dizziness, or syncope.
• Psychiatric: Denies anxiety, depression, or insomnia.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• Constitutional: No signs of edema.
• Respiratory: Clear to auscultation and percussion bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi. Unlabored breathing.
• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs. No jugular venous distention. No carotid bruits.
• Skin: No rashes, lesions, or bruises. No hepatosplenomegaly.
• Psychiatric: Alert, pleasant, and in no acute distress. No evidence of anxiety, agitation, or depression.

ASSESSMENT

• Alcohol use disorder.
• Hypertension.
• Mild hypercholesterolemia.

PLAN

We discussed the potential for a medication to help with the alcohol use. The patient would like to start naltrexone. We will start her on the injectable form and start on 05/17/2021. We reviewed the side effects and risks, and we discussed the potential benefits of alcohol cessation. We also discussed the possibility that her blood pressure may improve completely with cessation of alcohol. She is taking lisinopril 10 mg daily and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily. Her target blood pressure is 130-140/70-80.

We will see her back week of 06/14/2021, which is likely around the time of her second naltrexone injection.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in the week of 06/14/2021.
