summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right upper quadrant pain.

MEDICAL HISTORY

Patient reports history of type 2 diabetes.

FAMILY HISTORY

Patient reports parents had gallbladder disease.

SOCIAL HISTORY

Patient reports that he is a photographer.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Reports low-grade fevers.
Gastrointestinal: Reports right upper quadrant pain, nausea.

VITALS

BP: 128/88.
Temp: Afebrile.
Pulse: 68.
RR: 16.
SpO2: 99% on room air.

PHYSICAL EXAM

CV: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopic beats, clicks, or murmurs noted.
Respiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally.
Abdominal: Positive bowel sounds. Soft, nondistended. Positive for some slight guarding to the right upper quadrant. No rebound tenderness.

RESULTS

Abdominal ultrasound shows multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. Mild thickening of the gallbladder wall. Common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.

ASSESSMENT

1. Cholecystitis with gallstones, without obstruction.
2. Type 2 diabetes.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and test results, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms and treatment options. At this time, I recommend a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. I explained to the patient that this procedure will take approximately 1-2 hours and he will need to spend the night in the hospital for observation. I also advised him to avoid high-fat foods post-operatively and to eat foods high in fiber. All questions were answered. We will follow up in 1 week post-operatively. He will follow up with his primary care physician for his type 2 diabetes.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 1 week post-operatively. }}<|eot_id|>
