summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right shoulder pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Matthew Smith is a 67-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of right shoulder pain. The onset of his pain began 4 days ago. The patient reports that he was playing tackle football and was tackled and landed on his right shoulder. He notes that his symptoms have been constant since the injury. He denies numbness or tingling in his fingers. He states that he is having difficulty lifting heavy objects and reaching overhead. He denies any issues with the strength of his arm. He reports that his pain is worse with certain movements. He has been taking Tylenol with limited relief.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports right shoulder pain. Denies left shoulder pain, neck pain, or other joint pain.
Neurological: Denies numbness or tingling in the fingers. Denies headaches or dizziness.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Neurological: Alert and oriented x3. No focal deficits.
Musculoskeletal: Examination of the right shoulder: Pain to palpation of the posterior shoulder at the AC joint. Decreased abduction and adduction to 90 degrees. Pain with supination and pronation of the right arm. Palpable radial artery pulse.

RESULTS

X-ray images of the right shoulder were obtained and reviewed in the office today. These reveal an acute AC joint fracture.

ASSESSMENT

Acute AC joint fracture.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have recommended that we treat him conservatively with a sling. I have prescribed Meloxicam 15 mg, once a day. We will order a CT scan of his right shoulder to further evaluate the AC joint. We will also refer him to physical therapy to strengthen his shoulder in hopes of avoiding surgery in the future. All questions were answered.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks for re-evaluation. }}<|eot_id|>
