HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Scott Miller is a pleasant 34-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of a right knee injury. The patient states that he was trying to go out and play some pickup soccer games over the weekend and went to go plant kick the ball and felt a pop in his right knee. He states that the symptoms started about 3 days ago. He notes that he has some effusion that has resolved. He states that his knee is just not functioning as it used to be. He notes that he has some limited range of motion and that he is unable to bear weight on it without discomfort. He states that he is able to bend his knee but there is pain towards the end of the motion. He states that he is able to straighten his knee but it does not feel like he used to. He states that he is able to bear weight on it but it feels loose. He states that the pain is between a 6 and 7 out of 10. He states that he is taking anti-inflammatories, which has helped a little bit, but he is on it regularly.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain and limited range of motion.

PHYSICAL EXAM

MSK: Examination of the right knee: I do appreciate some significant effusion in the right knee. There is slight ecchymosis. There is pain to palpation at the medial joint line. The patient is unable to perform a full range of motion. He is lacking a few degrees of terminal extension to about 115 degrees. He is lacking about 20 to 30 degrees of terminal flexion. There is a positive Lachman exam.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Right knee injury, likely anterior cruciate ligament tear.

Medical Reasoning: The patient's examination is suggestive of an anterior cruciate ligament tear. He has significant effusion, ecchymosis, pain to palpation, limited range of motion, and a positive Lachman exam. We will obtain an MRI to further evaluate the integrity of the ACL.

