summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Follow-up for acute on chronic CHF.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

The patient is an 82-year-old male with a past medical history significant for hypertension and stage 3 chronic kidney disease who presents for hospital follow-up after an acute on chronic CHF exacerbation. He reports that he has been watching his diet and noticed swelling in his ankles. He notes that one day he became short of breath and had to call 9-1-1. He states that when the paramedics arrived, they placed him on oxygen. The patient states that the emergency room doctor stated that his blood pressure was elevated.

The patient states that he has been trying to take his medications but sometimes misses a dose or two. He reports that he is a big cheese fan and loves watching football and going to parties on weekends. He states that he enjoys pizza and chicken wings and is having difficulty avoiding these items.

The patient reports that he has been feeling well. He states that he has been to the hospital for a couple of days and was given a medication that caused him to urinate frequently. He states that he was also given a water pill through his IV and was placed on different medications for his blood pressure. He reports that he feels much better now.

The patient states that he has been watching his diet and taking his medications since his discharge. He reports that he purchased a blood pressure cuff at home and has been checking his blood pressure every other day. He states that his blood pressure has been good. He denies shortness of breath, chest pain, or difficulty sleeping. He reports that he is able to get up and move around and does not have any trouble. He states that he does have an afternoon nap.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Denies fever, chills, or weight loss.
• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain.
• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.
• Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain.
• Genitourinary: Denies dysuria or hematuria.

PHYSICAL EXAMINATION

• Neck
- General Examination: No jugular venous distention.

• Respiratory
- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.

• Cardiovascular
- Auscultation of Heart: Grade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur, stable.

• Musculoskeletal
- Examination of Lower Extremities: Trace edema.

RESULTS

Echocardiogram reveals a preserved ejection fraction of 55%, abnormal diastolic filling, and mild to moderate mitral regurgitation.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

The patient is an 82-year-old male with a past medical history significant for hypertension and stage 3 chronic kidney disease who presents for follow-up after an acute on chronic CHF exacerbation.

• Acute on chronic combined systolic (congestive) heart failure.
- Medical Reasoning: This was caused by dietary indiscretion and uncontrolled hypertension.
- Medical Treatment: Continue Bumex 2 mg daily, diet modification, avoid salty foods, weigh daily and call if a 3-pound weight gain in 2 days. Referral to a nutritionist for dietary education.

• Hypertension.
- Medical Reasoning: Patient has a history of hypertension and has been non-compliant with his medications.
- Medical Treatment: Continue Cozaar 100 mg daily, continue Norvasc 5 mg daily, renal artery ultrasound.

• Stage 3 chronic kidney disease.
- Medical Reasoning: Patient has a history of stage 3 chronic kidney disease.
- Additional Testing: Blood work to assess renal function.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>
