summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right ankle pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Gregory Smith is a pleasant 35-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right ankle pain. The onset of his symptoms began yesterday after he fell on the ice. The patient states that he was walking to take out the trash and slipped on a patch of ice. He reports that he was unable to bear weight on the right ankle initially but now he is able to bear weight with some pain. The patient states that he has been keeping his ankle elevated, icing it, and taking ibuprofen, which has provided him with some relief. The patient denies any numbness in his right foot. He has a history of playing soccer and has been playing in a community league. He is currently watching his children play soccer.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports right ankle pain and swelling.
Neurological: Denies right ankle numbness.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Vital signs: Stable.
Skin: Ecchymosis over the lateral malleolus of the right ankle.
MSK: Examination of the right ankle: Tenderness to palpation anterolaterally. No laxity on anterior drawer and inversion stress. No bony tenderness on palpation of the foot. Neurovascularly intact distally. Capillary refill is less than 3 seconds with strong dorsalis pedis pulse. Sensation is intact to light touch distally.

RESULTS

X-ray of the right ankle was reviewed and revealed no fracture.

ASSESSMENT

Right ankle sprain.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regard to his current symptoms. I have explained to the patient that his symptoms are consistent with a right ankle sprain. I have recommended that he keep his leg elevated and ice his ankle when he is seated. I have also recommended that he wear an Aircast for stabilization and use crutches for the next 1 to 2 days. He will then be able to start walking on it as tolerated. The patient will continue to take NSAIDs as needed for pain. I will follow up with him in 2 weeks to see how he is doing. }}<|eot_id|>
