summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Newly diagnosed hypercholesterolemia.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Julie Cruz is a 17-year-old non-binary, Hispanic female who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of newly diagnosed hypercholesterolemia. The patient was seen at her annual check-up where her cholesterol was screened via a finger prick test and it came back elevated. She reports that she is worried about this finding. The patient states that she has had her cholesterol checked before and it was borderline high. She denies any family history of hypercholesterolemia, but notes that she has a good relationship with her grandparents. She reports that she has been trying to eat a healthy diet, but she does enjoy eating chocolate chip cookies. She also enjoys going outside and riding her bike. The patient states that she is active during the week. She denies tobacco use, as she used to live with her parents who smoked, but she now lives on her own and no one smokes at her apartment. She denies drug use. She reports that she does not have any friends who smoke. She states that she has been feeling okay and has had health goals with her primary care physician.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Denies fevers, chills, weight loss, or weight gain.
• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or palpitations.
• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath or cough.
• Gastrointestinal: Denies nausea, vomiting, or abdominal pain.
• Musculoskeletal: Denies joint pain or muscle weakness.
• Neurological: Denies headaches or dizziness.
• Endocrine: Denies heat or cold intolerance.

PHYSICAL EXAMINATION

• Constitutional: Well-appearing, well-nourished, and in no acute distress.
• Cardiovascular: No murmurs, rubs, or gallops.
• Respiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally.
• Eyes: No xanthomas.
• Neck: No thyromegaly.
• Abdomen: No hepatomegaly or splenomegaly.

VITALS REVIEWED

• Blood pressure: Within normal limits.

RESULTS

The patient's cholesterol level was elevated at 210 mg/dL.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Julie Cruz is a 17-year-old non-binary, Hispanic female who presents for evaluation of newly diagnosed hypercholesterolemia.

• Hypercholesterolemia.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient's cholesterol level was elevated at 210 mg/dL. The patient denies a family history of hypercholesterolemia, but notes that she has had borderline high cholesterol in the past.
• Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed with the patient that there is a link between high cholesterol and premature heart attacks. I advised her that we will need to do some additional testing to determine if there are any other underlying causes for her high cholesterol. I also discussed with her the importance of a well-balanced diet and regular exercise.
• Medical Treatment: We will order a full cholesterol panel, as well as glucose, a complete metabolic panel, thyroid studies, and liver panels. Genetic testing will also be performed to see if this is familial or secondary. We will also follow up with a nutritionist to provide dietary recommendations.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

We will follow up in 3-6 months after she has met with the nutritionist and has instituted dietary changes. }}<|eot_id|>
