summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Bilateral knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Sophia Johnson is a 32-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of bilateral knee pain. The onset of her symptoms began 6 months ago. The pain is described as a deep achy pain that is located behind her kneecaps and is associated with a creaking sensation. The pain is exacerbated by activities such as getting from a seated position to standing and walking up and down stairs. She notes that the pain improves with rest. She denies any specific inciting event that caused the pain. She has tried ice, compression, and ibuprofen with minimal relief. She avoids taking medications as much as possible.

Ms. Johnson reports that she is active and enjoys running 5 to 6 miles per day. She has decreased her running distance to half a mile due to knee pain. She also enjoys watching television and working from home.

MEDICAL HISTORY

The patient denies any previous history of fractures or injuries.

SOCIAL HISTORY

The patient reports that she works from home. She is a runner and enjoys watching television. She is highly cooperative and proactive about her health.

MEDICATIONS

The patient reports taking ibuprofen and Aleve as needed.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports bilateral knee pain, deep achy pain behind the kneecaps, crepitus. Denies swelling or redness.

VITALS

Vitals reviewed and are within normal limits.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Neurological: Strength is 3/5 for abduction of the legs bilaterally. The remainder of muscle strength for the lower extremities is 5/5.
Respiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally.
Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs.
Musculoskeletal: Normal gait. There is no overlying erythema or ecchymosis. No effusion. Tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinaculum. Positive patellar grind test. Lachman, anterior and posterior drawer, and McMurray tests are all negative bilaterally.

RESULTS

X-rays of both knees obtained in office today are reviewed and are unremarkable. They show no fractures or osteoarthritis.

ASSESSMENT

Bilateral knee pain.

Diagnosis: Patellofemoral pain syndrome.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and radiographic findings, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to her current symptoms. Her symptoms are consistent with patellofemoral pain syndrome. I have recommended physical therapy to help strengthen her lower extremities, increase mobility, and correct any incorrect running mechanics. She will continue to take ibuprofen as needed for pain. She will follow up with me in the clinic in 4 weeks to assess her progress.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in 4 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>
